  * sends (count, mach_absolute_time, 0) as 32-bit halves to clients that
  * enabled it, through the port they registered (IOConnectSetNotificationPort).
  *
+ * GPU present (patch 0231, boot-arg negronifb_gpu, default 1): a swapped
+ * layer whose surface the GPU rendered (RedwoodGPU marks it at submission,
+ * NegroniSurface::gpuFence) is copied into the panel memory by the A730's 2D
+ * engine (NegroniGPUPresent), queued behind the render on the GPU's ring: no
+ * CPU copy and no wait, at every vsync tick (NEGRONI_FB_VSYNC_US, 120 Hz) or
+ * negronifb_gpuhz frames a second if that is lower.
+ * Its surface is held in use (IOSurfaceIsInUse, state page +0x18) from SwapEnd
+ * until the copy's fence is done, so CoreAnimation renders the next frame into
+ * another surface of its swap chain: the source side is double-buffered. The
+ * panel side is not: ABL's display engine keeps scanning its one buffer (a
+ * flip needs DPU programming, README-gpu-kernel.md), and the copy (well under
+ * a millisecond for 10 MB) can land mid-scan. Every other layer, and any frame
+ * the GPU cannot take, goes the CPU way below (negronifb_gpu=0: always), after
+ * the GPU's copies of the same frame and with the CPU's stale lines over a
+ * GPU-written surface dropped first.
+ *
  * The panel memory itself is published as an IOSurface and returned as the
  * default framebuffer surface. The kernel text console keeps drawing over
  * the GUI by default: boot-arg negronifb_noconsole=1 stops drawing it from
  * the first swap on (it still reaches the persistent log; panics draw).
  */
 #include "NegroniSurface.h"
+#include "NegroniGPU.h"
 
